---
// A mockup, not a decision: the bands view beside the two it would replace, on the real
// corpus, so the comparison is between three renderings of the same twenty-eight layers
// rather than between a screenshot and a memory. Delete this route with whichever loses.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LayerBands from '../../components/LayerBands.astro';
import PipelineExplorer from '../../components/PipelineExplorer';
import { corpusExplorer } from '../../lib/explorer';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const data = corpusExplorer(base);
---

<Layout title="Layer view — mockup" description="A proposed replacement for the pan-and-zoom layer map: depth as bands down the page.">
  <div class="site-frame column py-12">

    <header class="mb-10 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 m-0 mb-2">Mockup</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 tracking-tight m-0 mb-3">The layers, as bands</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 m-0 leading-relaxed">
        Both views on <a href={`${base}/pipeline/`} class="text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 no-underline hover:underline">the pipeline page</a>
        spend the screen's scarce axis on the pipeline's long one. Columns put nine depths
        across the width and overflow to 1680px, so two thirds of the map is off-screen.
        The graph fits by zooming out, which buys the shape at the cost of being able to read
        any node in it, and then needs pan and zoom to give that back.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3 m-0 leading-relaxed">
        Depth is an order, and the page has an unbounded axis for orders: down. Turning the
        graph a quarter turn makes the long axis scroll-the-page, which is free, and spends
        the width on the layers within a depth — which have no order among themselves and can
        wrap. Nothing is off-screen, nothing needs fitting, and a phone and a desktop differ
        only in how many chips sit on a line.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1">Proposed</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-5 max-w-2xl">
        No zoom, no pan, no sideways scroll, no canvas. The arrows are gone because a DAG laid
        out by depth carries direction in its layout — every edge points from a lower band to a
        higher one — so drawing them adds crossings and no information. What the arrows were
        for is a click instead: pick a layer and it marks what feeds it, and everything that
        falls out of date when it changes.
      </p>
      <LayerBands data={data} />
    </section>

    <section class="full mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1">What it would replace</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-5 max-w-2xl">
        The same twenty-eight layers, as the page renders them today. Scroll the columns
        sideways to see depth 5 onward, or switch to the graph and try to read a node.
      </p>
      <PipelineExplorer client:load data={data} base={base} height={470} />
    </section>

    <footer class="pt-6 border-t border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-800 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
      <p class="m-0">
        If the bands win, <code>LayerMap.tsx</code>, <code>LayerColumns.tsx</code>,
        <code>PipelineExplorer.tsx</code> and <code>useFitOnReveal.ts</code> go with the
        decision, and this page ships no JavaScript framework at all — the bands are an Astro
        component and forty lines of script. <code>reactflow</code> stays in the project for
        <code>ClaimDAG</code>, the claim graph on the paper pages, but stops being loaded here.
      </p>
    </footer>

  </div>
</Layout>
